15 0 obj
<<
/A 39 0 R
/Alt (Panel A shows a line graph comparing blood glucose concentration over time in Mus musculus and Acomys cahirinus during a glucose tolerance test, with Acomys cahirinus showing significantly lower glucose levels. Panel B presents a bar graph of the area under the curve for the glucose tolerance test, revealing a statistically significant lower value for Acomys cahirinus. Panel C displays a line graph of blood glucose levels during an insulin tolerance test, with Acomys cahirinus showing higher early response. Panel D contains a bar graph of the area under the curve for the insulin tolerance test, showing no significant difference between species.)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 30 0 R
/S /Figure
/T ()
>>
endobj
16 0 obj
<<
/A 40 0 R
/Alt (Panel A shows fluorescent microscopy images comparing pancreatic islets in Mus musculus and Acomys cahirinus, with insulin in red, glucagon in green, and nuclei in blue. Panel B presents a bar graph showing islets area is larger in Acomys cahirinus than Mus musculus. Panel C bar graph shows islet density per area is higher in Acomys cahirinus. Panel D depicts a bar graph indicating greater area of glucagon-producing cells in Acomys cahirinus. Panel E shows a bar graph with increased area of insulin-producing cells in Acomys cahirinus, all comparisons statistically significant with p values less than zero point zero five.)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 31 0 R
/S /Figure
/T ()
>>
endobj
17 0 obj
<<
/A 41 0 R
/Alt (Five scientific charts compare hepatic glucose production ex vivo and gluconeogenesis from different substrates in Mus musculus \(black\) and Acomys cahirinus \(orange\). Bar and line graphs show statistically significant differences \(p<0.05\) in glucose production and blood glucose dynamics between species for glucose, pyruvate, glycerol, and alanine time courses, and area under the curve \(AUC\) summary quantification. Error bars indicate standard error.)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 32 0 R
/S /Figure
/T ()
>>
endobj
18 0 obj
<<
/A 42 0 R
/Alt (Six-panel scientific figure comparing metabolic measures between Mus musculus and Acomys cahirinus. Panel A shows higher glucose uptake in epididymal fat for Acomys cahirinus. Panel B indicates no significant plasma lactate difference in fed animals, but higher lactate in Mus musculus after fasting. Panel C shows greater lactate in subcutaneous fat in Mus musculus under both conditions. Panel D presents significantly higher lactate in epididymal fat from fed Mus musculus. Panel E displays no significant difference in ^14C-labeled water soluble metabolites between groups. Panel F reveals higher ^14C-labeled lipids in Mus musculus for both fat types. Error bars and significance values are marked.)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 33 0 R
/S /Figure
/T ()
>>
endobj
19 0 obj
<<
/A 43 0 R
/Alt (Panel A shows liver tissue histological images for Mus musculus and Acomys cahirinus under fed and 24-hour fasting conditions, with stains highlighting glycogen and lipid content. Panels B through E display bar graphs quantifying glycogen and total lipid stained area in both species, showing significant differences \(p<0.05\). Panels F and G present biochemical assay results for total lipids, demonstrating higher lipid levels in Acomys cahirinus after fasting and higher levels in Mus musculus when fed.)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 34 0 R
/S /Figure
/T ()
>>
endobj
